# Isolated Native Audit Fixture

Build from the repository root (no root CMake changes):

    cmake -S docs/design/fixture -B build/design-audit -G "Visual Studio 17 2022" -A x64
    cmake --build build/design-audit --config Release --target audit_preview

Executable: build/design-audit/Release/audit_preview.exe
Nonvisual verification: audit_preview.exe --self-test

This target compiles the real src/app SettingsWindow, General page and About
window, and links the existing build/ci-vs2022-x64 Release module/UI libraries.
It embeds the real resource icon, built language pack and application manifest.
It does not rebuild production libraries. input-provenance.txt in the build
directory records configure-time SHA-256 fingerprints of these existing inputs;
reconfigure after the parent changes/rebuilds them. Mixed source/library revisions
are possible and must be considered when interpreting screenshots.

Historical menu comparison
--------------------------
SIMPILOT_AUDIT_MENU_RENDERER may point to an unmodified historical
launch_menu_renderer.cpp extracted from a Git tag under build/. Configure each
variant into a separate build directory. That translation unit is linked into
the fixture executable instead of the renderer object in the production static
library; the public renderer header must remain ABI-compatible. The source hash
is included in input-provenance.txt. Omit the option for the current renderer.
This captures real historical renderer code using identical current fixture
data and infrastructure. It is NOT evidence of running the complete old release.
No historical override is available in the product's CMake or executable.

Surfaces
--------
- Eight real settings pages: General, Quick Launch, Menu Icons, Global Hotkeys
  (including custom hotkeys), Keyboard Mappings, Everything, Cursor Locator,
  Windows Shortcuts.
- Separate fresh settings variants: full/default 16, empty 0, few 3, many 40
  custom-hotkey AND keyboard-mapping rows. Mixed enabled states, long target
  paths, bilingual labels, action types, mapping purposes and scoped processes.
  Empty means empty collections, not an empty settings-page registry.
- Real standalone CustomHotKeyDialog and KeyboardMappingDialog, add/edit.
  Standalone results are discarded; dialogs inside settings update real drafts.
- Real ProgramSelectionDialog with four safe mock executable copies. Candidate
  versions are simulated; paths and file times belong to actual fixture files.
- Real AboutWindow with explicitly simulated version text; external links denied.
- Real QuickLaunchModule main/second menus, nested categories, separators,
  application/document/folder targets, actual menu icon cache and owner-draw
  renderer. Invoked through real hotkey contribution callbacks, not global keys.
  System/light/dark theme buttons select fresh module instances.
- Tray popup uses real module TrayMenuRegistry contributions and native HMENU,
  including disabled Everything actions and language/group submenus.
  IMPORTANT GAP: the TrayApplication host is private and starts production
  infrastructure, so this host assembly is recreated, NOT production tray-host
  screenshot evidence. Tray selections are observed but not dispatched.
- Real CursorLocator over a fixture-owned light/dark background. Manually shake
  the pointer to trigger the actual detector and time-limited animation; closing
  the background stops sampling. No synthetic movement or frozen fake effect.

Manual Audit
------------
Use zh-CN/en-US buttons before opening a surface. They apply to subsequent
surfaces; the real General language selector also works during settings.
Select fake commit success/failure before opening settings, change a control,
then use real Apply/Save. Failure invokes real participant rollback; success
keeps session state and writes only a candidate INI under that variant's temp
directory. Quick-launch changes use the real temporary menu/icon transaction.
Start-with-Windows and shortcut blocking are drafts/simulations, not OS changes.
Each settings button creates a fresh variant, deliberately discarding prior data.
Default launcher state is 16 rows; menu/dialog buttons lazily create that fixture.

The read-only launcher panel identifies simulation/gaps, active language, commit
outcome, row count and launcher DPI. Each run has a GUID-named directory under
the OS temp directory. metadata.ini updates once per second, including visible
current-thread window titles/classes/rectangles/client sizes/DPI, executable,
build configuration and active variant path. Rectangles are pixels, not DIPs.
This is read-only window inspection, not screenshot capture. Temp files are left
for the parent to inspect and remove explicitly; no broad temp cleanup is done.

Safety And Evidence Limits
--------------------------
- KeyboardManager is constructed but NEVER started or asked to register keys.
  Mapping stages exist in memory only. Recording and foreground-process capture
  are unsupported gaps. Do not present unavailable recording as recorded input.
- Everything.exe/Everything64.dll are absent from the isolated root. The real
  unavailable/disabled page is shown; search and service installation are gaps.
- No personal settings are loaded. All settings/menu inputs are created with
  strongly typed production codecs and MenuWriter; copies of THIS fixture binary
  serve only as local icon/selection targets. They are never executed.
- The command_execution library is intentionally omitted. The fixture supplies
  inert execute_command and temp-only user_profile_directory boundaries.
- shell32.dll is delay-loaded. The fixture's native delay-import notification
  hook denies ShellExecuteW/ShellExecuteExW, including About links, without
  changing production source or rendering. This is NOT a Windows input hook.
  Blocked About links show the real failure message, not simulated success.
- No StartupRegistration call, keyboard hook thread, RegisterHotKey call, service
  install, command dispatch, screenshot API, UI-driving code or personal config.
  The real quick-launch config watcher watches only fixture files and is joined
  when its module stops. Cursor locator uses its real timer/GetCursorPos sampling.
- Native file pickers remain real: defaults/seeded inputs point to fixture data.
  Manually navigating elsewhere is outside the controlled audit dataset.
- About layout and module UI are real, but version/installed-program metadata,
  keyboard availability and commit outcomes are not production-state evidence.
- Default builds compile no temporary production-source copies or replacement
  renderers. The explicitly selected historical comparison option above is the
  only exception and is recorded separately from current-product evidence.

--self-test validates all four typed datasets and menu round trips, eight-page
registration, module start/stop, real commit-failure rollback and commit success,
unstarted keyboard state, and both denied shell entry points. It does not create
settings/dialog windows, drive controls, capture screenshots or certify layout.
Results are in the run's self-test-results.ini (or failure.ini on failure).
The parent owns all manual UI/screenshot/design audits.
